#errorCatcher Echo
##
## archive.json.tmpl — flat JSON snapshot of current/today/trend observation
## data, converted from the real dvmCombinedData PHP module. This replaces
## that PHP file's job for the front-end: instead of the JS guessing units
## from charts.json's hourly series (which is how the wind-speed/gust bug
## happened — those fields come through in the report's configured display
## unit, not bare m/s, and the front-end was assuming m/s), every value
## here goes through WeeWX's own $obj.format(...)/$unit.label tags, so it's
## already correctly converted to the report's real unit system. No more
## guessing.
##
## Deliberate differences from the original PHP, flagged rather than done
## quietly:
##   1. Missing-data fields use the JSON literal `null` (None_string="null")
##      instead of the PHP version's "0" — "0" reads as a real zero
##      reading, which isn't true when a sensor has no data at all.
##   2. Every *_time / *_maxtime / *_mintime field is a raw unix_epoch_ms
##      integer, not a server-formatted string like PHP's date('H:i', ...).
##      The PHP baked one fixed format per field (some 'H:i', some
##      'D j M H:i', some 'j M Y H:i') — formatting that client-side from
##      a raw timestamp is more flexible and matches how charts.json
##      already works throughout the rest of this project.
##   3. Found and fixed a real bug in the original barometer trend
##      cascade: the negative-side checks were ordered <0, then <-1, then
##      <-2, then <-3 — since <0 catches every negative value first and
##      the chain stops on first match, "Falling", "Falling Quickly" and
##      "Falling Very Rapidly" could never actually be reached; everything
##      negative collapsed to "Falling Slowly". Reordered to check the
##      most extreme case first, mirroring how the positive side already
##      did it correctly.
##   4. Fixed a second real bug, in the rain block: the "event" field's
##      $current.eventRain.format(...) call sat OUTSIDE the #try/#except
##      that was meant to guard storm_start_val above it. When eventRain
##      threw (not a recognised obs type for this binding), #errorCatcher
##      Echo's own placeholder syntax — ?'eventRain'? — leaked straight
##      into the JSON output as a bare token, which isn't valid JSON and
##      broke parsing of the entire file for every consumer. The format()
##      call now lives inside the same #try, with a safe "0" default set
##      beforehand, exactly like storm_start_val's own fallback.
##
## Page-theme/almanac concerns from the original file (ephem sun/moon
## ecliptic calculations, daylight-length, day/night body theme) are left
## out on purpose — they're not observation data, and astronomical.json
## already covers that ground (see the project's continuity notes). The
## trailing PHP includes (dvmColorMap.php, dvmAlmanacData.php,
## dvmUnitConversions.php, the commented-out meteor showers include) are
## likewise out of scope for an observations feed.
##
#import time
#import math
#import platform
##
## safeMs — guards every int(raw * 1000) timestamp conversion below.
## $X.raw can legitimately come back None (derived/extension obs types like
## vpd don't always track maxtime/mintime; any obs can be None if a station
## has never recorded a value for that aggregation window) and int(None*1000)
## crashes the whole template generation per the archive.json ERROR log —
## Cheetah's #errorCatcher Echo only guards individual $var placeholders,
## not arithmetic inside a $(...) Python expression block, so one None here
## takes out the entire file rather than just this one field.
#def safeMs($val)
#if $val is not None
$int($val * 1000)
#else
null
#end if
#end def
##
## Fixed this session (beta report, Gert -- station in Kongens Lyngby,
## Denmark): every numeric field in this file used
## .format(format_string=..., None_string=..., add_label=False) WITHOUT
## localize=False. WeeWX's ValueHelper.format() calls Python's
## locale.format_string() internally when the report/skin's configured
## language (weewx.conf's [StdReport][[...]] lang setting, or the active
## system locale) uses a decimal comma instead of a decimal point --
## Danish (da_DK), German, French, and most of continental Europe do.
## That's the CORRECT behaviour for a human-facing report page (the
## Climate Report, the dashboard cards, etc. all want "1,2214" for a
## Danish reader) but it's exactly WRONG for this file: archive.json is
## machine-readable JSON, and "1,2214" isn't a JSON number -- it's three
## separate tokens, which corrupted parsing of the ENTIRE file for every
## consumer. Since every single numeric field here went through
## .format(), every one of them corrupted at once as soon as the skin's
## locale was anything other than a decimal-point one -- that's why the
## bug hit literally every number in the file simultaneously.
##
## The fix is minimal and idiomatic: WeeWX's own .format() already
## supports a localize=False argument specifically for this -- it was
## already being used correctly, once, on the
## alltime.lightning_distance.lastnonzero line below. Added it to the
## other 144 .format() calls in this file that were missing it, rather
## than inventing a workaround.
##
## This also incidentally fixes a second, hidden bug: five places in this
## file (baromTrendRaw, dewTrendRaw, humidTrendRaw, indoorTrendRaw,
## outTempTrendRaw) built a trend value via the same .format() call and
## then re-parsed it with Python's float() to classify rising/falling/
## steady. Under a comma-decimal locale, float("0,1234") raises
## ValueError -- which their own #try/#except was silently swallowing,
## always falling back to trend=0 ("Steady"). That's very likely why
## Gert's barom.trend_code showed 0/"Steady" regardless of the station's
## real pressure trend. With localize=False, .format()'s output is
## guaranteed to use '.' as the decimal separator, so float() on it now
## works correctly without needing any separate fallback path.
##
